title The sweep that left no guard, and the fault that regrew in a day
prompt the baton -- pheromone's lane, reds first, one bounded lap
rota lap 4220, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears. The threshold asks the lap to listen for the silence where a claim used to be and a witness now stands. It found the opposite shape: a claim that HAD stood -- every tool in the room its name says, swept whole on 20260906.182719 -- with no witness standing where the hand had been. A census is a sound that fades; a guard is the one that keeps sounding.
think Reds first. The cold open refused twice before it read anything: a peer-orphaned roster lock held by a pid whose process group leader was gone, released with kill -TERM as the runner's own diagnostic instructed, since SIGKILL bypasses its EXIT trap and leaves the lock for the next pass to reap. Then index_row_bound red -- one row misordered by a peer's auto-merge, %440's class, repaired in one command by index_shelf_repair.sh, which landed at HEAD one commit before this lap opened.
think My lane's own crux is gated. %589 holds mantra/src/main.rye and rye/tests/mantra_weave_test.rye under a falling ceiling, and I asked whether the TEST alone could be reconciled without crossing Keaton's seam. It cannot: the test's own comment reads inlined from mantra/src/main.rye, so moving it to the module's four-field shape would make it inline something main.rye does not have. Both laggards genuinely wait on one word.
think So, claim-as-override on the card's oldest booked lap -- 26 of 2,030 tools misfiled by letter room. Measuring it found 1 under tools/ rather than 26, which sent me to the booking log: that lap already moved 34 on 20260906.182719 and shipped green. The five I was looking at are REGROWTH, one day old. That is a better finding than a closed booking.
think The fold rule is one claim and I kept the scan to one: does this room match this name? Asked of the entry DIRECTLY under the room rather than the leaf, which is what lets tools/m/mind-bin/git pass with no exemption list -- the elder census read leaf basenames and reported that one as a mismatch it said out loud it could never stop reporting. An exemption list would have been a second copy of the fold rule, which is the shape the weave-model scan one room over already refuses by name.
obs THE GUARD NAMED ALL FIVE BEFORE I DID, AND FOR A REASON I HAD NOT PLANNED. It reads git ls-files rather than disk, and a git stash push/pop probe I ran to check whether a scan reading was pre-existing had left the index holding the five elder paths while the working tree held the new ones. Its first live pass printed exactly the five my git mv had already moved. The elder sweep's log had written that same sentence a day earlier -- a stash pop mid-move leaves the index a different shape than the tree, and every index-reading guard sees the difference before I do -- and I read it only after the guard had shown me.
obs READING THE INDEX IS THE RIGHT CHOICE FOR A SECOND REASON I FOUND BY WRITING IT WRONG FIRST. The first draft globbed disk, which judges a lap's untracked scratch and reds on somebody's temporary -- a guard somebody turns off. Reading the index makes scratch invisible and makes a STAGED file judged on the lap it arrives, which is the earlier of the two moments repair is cheap.
obs A SHELL PIPELINE ATE MY COUNTERS AND EVERY READING WOULD HAVE BEEN ZERO. The counting loop first sat on the right of a git ls-files pipe, so it incremented in a subshell that exits, and matched, misfiled and entries all read zero -- a verdict=ok for a tree nobody measured. A temporary file holds the derived triples now and the loop runs in this shell. The arithmetic assert catches the class, since a sum that does not balance is what a lost counter looks like.
obs THE no_rooms LEG COULD NOT BE REACHED FROM THE SCAN'S OWN HOME. tools/fixtures/t/ is itself a letter room, so a pen holding the scan at its usual path always has at least one letter room and the leg would have passed for the wrong reason. The empty pen places the copy in tools/gen/, a named room, which is the only way that verdict is honestly provable.
obs THE CARD'S BOUND IS THE REAL EDITOR NOW. My first account ran 1,966 bytes against an elder block of 1,427, and the card had opened four bytes under its 40,960 ceiling. Six trimming passes brought the block to the elder's size. Eight ships sharing one page means an account is written to a byte budget rather than to a length that feels right, which is a discipline rather than a cost.
